3. NO-SELL RESTRICTION (LANGUAGE PRODUCTS)

3.1 Restriction. You may not Sell a Language Product unless you have
obtained a separate commercial license from the Licensor. This
restriction is meant to reach only products that sell the language
itself.

3.2 Language Product. A "Language Product" is a product or service,
offered to third parties, that is obtained so that Programs can be
written for it and checked, compiled, or run by the Software: without
its users' Programs, it would have no purpose of its own. Whatever
serves only to write, check, run, or grade Programs — editors, tooling,
and host function bindings (such as extern functions) that only
Programs call — is part of the language it sells, not a purpose of its
own, however much of it there is. For example:

  (a) These are Language Products: a compiler explorer, a playground,
      an online IDE, a notebook, an editor, or a language server; a
      service that hosts and runs its users' Programs; a scripting,
      rule, or template engine offered to developers; a game framework
      or engine in which game developers write their games as Programs;
      a coding test or contest whose submissions are Programs; a game
      in which writing Programs is itself the play; and the Software,
      or a language built on it, offered under another name.

  (b) These are not Language Products: a game, including one made with
      a framework built on the Software, and the mods its players
      write; an application in which user-written Programs are one
      feature among others; a platform where users play or converse
      with what others have scripted; and a product whose Programs are
      written by whoever offers it and whose users supply only Values —
      messages, settings, records, or uploaded data — even where the
      Software type-checks them, or where one of those Programs
      interprets text its users write.

3.3 Sell. "Sell" means to offer a Language Product to third parties for
any fee, subscription, license charge, advertising revenue, or other
compensation, whether or not the offering is profitable, or to offer it
free of charge as part of, bundled with, or to promote something for
which compensation is received. Voluntary donations on which nothing
depends are not compensation. Tooling offered free of charge to write
Programs for a product that is not a Language Product, or to teach the
Software, is not Sold.

3.4 Permitted Uses. For clarity, the following are permitted without a
commercial license, including in commercial contexts:

  (a) using the Software in any product or service that is not a
      Language Product;

  (b) selling or otherwise commercially exploiting User Content,
      including output produced by the Software;

  (c) internal use within an organization, including use by its
      employees and contractors acting on its behalf;

  (d) being paid to author User Content for another party, or to build
      a work that uses the Software for a single client, which owns it
      and uses it only internally, provided that you do not offer that
      work, or one substantially the same, to anyone else; and

  (e) offering or distributing a Language Product free of charge,
      subject to Sections 5 and 6.

3.5 Questions. If you are unsure whether what you offer is a Language
Product, ask the Licensor (Section 12).

3.6 Scope. This Section applies to the Software wherever it is
included, regardless of the license under which any other part of a
product or service is distributed.


